.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_mctx
is a hierarchical memory context, modeled on PostgreSQL's
.Vt MemoryContext .
Allocations belong to a context; destroying the context bulk-frees
everything in it
.Pq plus all child contexts recursively .
Useful for request-scoped memory: create a context per request,
allocate freely, destroy at the end without tracking individual
frees.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_mctx_create
takes a
.Fa flags
bitmask of
.Dv XTC_MCTX_*
values:
.Dv XTC_MCTX_DEFAULT
.Pq 0
for an unlocked context, or
.Dv XTC_MCTX_THREAD_SAFE
for one that is internally locked.
A
.Dv NULL
.Fa parent
produces a root context;
.Fa name
is copied for diagnostics.
.Pp
Each context can register cleanup callbacks fired on destroy
.Pq before the bulk-free
to release non-memory resources.
.Pp
No context lock is held while a cleanup callback runs, so a callback may
call back into its own context
.Pq Fn xtc_mctx_total_bytes , Fn xtc_mctx_total_chunks , or an allocation .
What it must NOT do is reset or destroy the very context running it.
Callbacks are detached before they run, so each fires exactly once even
if the context is reset concurrently; register again if the next reset
should call it too.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_mctx_alloc
returns raw memory within the context;
.Fn xtc_mctx_calloc
zeroes an
.Fa n
by
.Fa size
array; and
.Fn xtc_mctx_strdup
copies string
.Fa s
into the context.  All live until the context is reset or
destroyed.
.Fn xtc_mctx_free
optionally releases a single allocation early.
.Fn xtc_mctx_register_cleanup
adds a callback
.Fa fn
run in LIFO order at reset/destroy.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_mctx_name
returns the context's name;
.Fn xtc_mctx_total_bytes
and
.Fn xtc_mctx_total_chunks
report the memory and chunk count currently charged to the
context.
.Ss Arena groups: wholesale shared-state discard on kill
An arena group binds a set of member fibers to one discardable arena,
giving a recovery boundary smaller than the whole process -- the
analogue of a crash-only database reinitialising shared memory.
.Fn xtc_arena_group_create
makes an empty group with a fresh thread-safe arena, and
.Fn xtc_arena_group_destroy
frees it (it does not kill members).
.Fn xtc_arena_group_mctx
returns the arena; members allocate their SHARED state there, and
.Fn xtc_arena_group_add
registers the calling fiber as a member.
.Fn xtc_arena_group_discard
is the point of the facility: it
.Fn xtc_exit_pid_deadline Ns s
every live member, waits until ALL are confirmed gone, and only THEN
resets the arena -- so the shared state a killed fiber was mid-mutating
is thrown away with nothing alive still touching it.  If a member is
wedged inside a mask (kill deferred) and still alive at its deadline,
the arena is left INTACT and the caller is told
.Va all_gone
== 0, so it escalates to the whole-process fail-stop rather than
discard state a live fiber may still touch.
.Fn xtc_arena_group_size
reports the current member count.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_mctx_t *root, *req;
xtc_mctx_create(NULL, "root", XTC_MCTX_DEFAULT, &root);
xtc_mctx_create(root, "request-42", XTC_MCTX_DEFAULT, &req);

/* Allocate request-scoped objects. */
char *buf = xtc_mctx_alloc(req, 4096);
struct widget *w = xtc_mctx_alloc(req, sizeof *w);

/* Done; bulk-free everything in req. */
xtc_mctx_destroy(req);
.Ed
